      "output": "Title: Built-in Functions — Python 3.14.7 documentation\nURL: https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n| A `abs()` `aiter()` `all()` `anext()` `any()` `ascii()` B `bin()` `bool()` `breakpoint()` `bytearray()` `bytes()` C `callable()` `chr()` `classmethod()` `compile()` `complex()` D `delattr()` `dict()` `dir()` `divmod()` | E `enumerate()` `eval()` `exec()` F `filter()` `float()` `format()` `frozenset()` G `getattr()` `globals()` H `hasattr()` `hash()` `help()` `hex()` I `id()` `input()` `int()` `isinstance()` `issubclass()` `iter()` | L `len()` `list()` `locals()` M `map()` `max()` `memoryview()` `min()` N `next()` O `object()` `oct()` `open()` `ord()` P `pow()` `print()` `property()` | R `range()` `repr()` `reversed()` `round()` S `set()` `setattr()` `slice()` `sorted()` `staticmethod()` `str()` `sum()` `super()` T `tuple()` `type()` V `vars()` Z `zip()` _ `__import__()` |\n\n---\n\nTitle: What Is Python's sum() Function and How Do You Use It? - The New Stack\nURL: https://thenewstack.io/what-is-pythons-sum-function-and-how-do-you-use-it/\nPublished: 2024-08-11T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Jack Wallen\nHighlights:\nHowever, there’s a built-in function that’s much better to use. The function in question is `sum()`. One of the primary reasons for using this function is readability. Instead of having to write `for` loops or recursions, `sum()` takes care of all that for you.\n...\nThe `sum()` function can be used with lists, tuples, sets, ranges and dictionaries.\n...\nshow you how each might look:\n...\n- List: sum([3, 5, 7, 10])\n- Tuple: sum((3, 5, 7, 10))\n- Set: sum({3, 5, 7, 10})\n- Range: sum(range(3, 10))\n- Dictionary: sum({3: “three”, 5: “five”, 7: “seven”, 10: “ten”})\n...\nWith the exception of the range, all of the above will total 25. The range, on the other hand, will total 42.\n...\nYou could also get a bit more fancy with your math. For example, let’s say you want to compute the sum of the squares from a range of values. That might look something like this:\n...\n```\nsum([x ** 2 for x in range(1, 10)])\n```\n...\nThe total from the above would be 285.\n...\nThe `sum()` function has two arguments: the required iterable (the numbers to be iterated through) and the optional `start`. The `start` argument comes in very handy. Let’s say you want to add a list of iterables together but you want to start out with a predefined starting value. Without the `start` argument, the starting value is always assumed to be 0. Take a look at the following line:\n\n---\n\nTitle: Built-in Functions — Python 3.11.5 documentation\nURL: https://docs.python.org/release/3.11.5/library/functions.html\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n`range()`\n...\n`sum()`\n...\nsum(iterable, /, start=0)¶\n\n---\n\nTitle: Predict the output of the following code: python sum = 0 for p in range(..\nURL: https://askfilo.com/user-question-answers-smart-solutions/predict-the-output-of-the-following-code-python-sum-0-for-p-3337333034383031\nPublished: 2025-08-24T12:59:38.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n```\nsum = 0\nfor p in range(1, 11):\n    sum = sum + p\n...\nprint(\"sum of natural number from 1 to 10\", sum)\n\n```\n...\nThe code initializes a variable`sum` to 0. Then, it loops through the numbers from 1 to 10 (inclusive) with the range function`range(1, 11)`. For each number`p`, it adds`p` to the current value of`sum`. After the loop completes,`sum` holds the sum of the natural numbers from 1 to 10.\n...\nInitialize`sum` to 0.\n...\nIterate over each number`p` from 1 to 10:\n...\n- When`p=1`, sum = 0 + 1 = 1\n- When`p=2`, sum = 1 + 2 = 3\n- When`p=3`, sum = 3 + 3 = 6\n- When`p=4`, sum = 6 + 4 = 10\n- When`p=5`, sum = 10 + 5 = 15\n- When`p=6`, sum = 15 + 6 = 21\n- When`p=7`, sum = 21 + 7 = 28\n- When`p=8`, sum = 28 + 8 = 36\n- When`p=9`, sum = 36 + 9 = 45\n- When`p=10`, sum = 45 + 10 = 55\n...\nAfter the loop, print the result using the statement:\n...\n```\nprint(\"sum of natural number from 1 to 10\", sum)\n\n```\n...\nThis outputs:`sum of natural number from 1 to 10 55`\n...\nsum of natural number from 1 to 10 55\n\n---\n\nTitle: पायथन में अनुक्रम के सभी सदस्यों का योग - sum विधि\nURL: https://code.mu/hi/python/manual/math/sum/\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nपायथन\n...\nसभी सदस्यों का योग - sum\n...\n# sum विधि\n\n `sum` विधि संख्याओं के अनुक्रम के सभी सदस्यों का योग लौटाती है। लौटाए गए मान का प्रकार जोड़ी जा रही संख्याओं पर निर्भर करता है।\n...\n## वाक्य-विन्यास\n\n `sum(संख्याओं का अनुक्रम)`\n...\n## उदाहरण \n\n आइए सूची में पूर्णांकों के अनुक्रम के सभी सदस्यों का योग ज्ञात करें: \n\n `lst = [1, 2, 3]\n...\nprint(sum(lst))` \n\n कोड निष्पादित करने का परिणाम: \n\n `6`\n\n---\n\nTitle: sum() | Python’s Built-in Functions – Real Python\nURL: https://realpython.com/ref/builtin-functions/sum/\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: Real Python\nHighlights:\nThe built-in`sum()` function provides a Pythonic way to add together the items of an iterable. It efficiently computes the total sum of numeric values, with the option to include an initial value for the summation process:\n...\n3, 4,\n...\nsum([1, 2,\n...\n3, 4,\n...\n5], start\n...\n100)\n...\n```\nsum(iterable, /, start=0)\n...\n| Argument | Description | Default Value |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `iterable` | Any Python iterable containing numeric values. It can also hold concatenable objects, such as lists and tuples. | Required argument |\n| `start` | An optional argument to initialize the summation. It can be a number, list, or tuple, but not a string. | `0` |\n...\n- The sum of the`start` value and the values in the input iterable from left to right.\n- The return value’s data type matches the type of the elements in the input iterable.\n...\n```\n>>> sum([x ** 2 for x in range(1, 6)])\n55\n...\n2, 3\n...\n- Lists vs Tuples in Python(Tutorial)\n- Numbers in Python(Tutorial)\n- Representing Rational Numbers With Python Fractions(Tutorial)\n- Python range(): Represent Numerical Ranges(Tutorial)\n...\n- Summing Values the Pythonic Way With sum()(Course)\n- Lists and Tuples\n...\nPython(Course)\n- Lists vs Tuples in Python(Quiz)\n- The Python range() Function(Course)\n\n---\n\nTitle: sum() in Python - Built-In Functions with Examples\nURL: https://diveintopython.org/functions/built-in/sum\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nThe `sum()` function is a built-in function in Python that takes an iterable (such as a list) of numbers as input and returns the sum of all the numbers in the iterable.\n...\n| Parameter | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| iterable | An iterable (list, tuple, set, etc.) of numeric values that will be summed up. |\n| start | An optional parameter that specifies the value to start the sum with. By default, it is 0. |\n...\nExample 1: \n\nReturn the sum of a 'list' or 'tuple' of numbers.\n...\n```python3\nnumbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n...\nresult = sum(numbers)\nprint(result)\n...\nExample 3: \n\nCalculate the sum of numbers in a 'generator expression'.\n...\n```python3\ngenerator = (x**2 for x in range(5))\nresult = sum(generator)\nprint(result)\n\n---\n\nTitle: sum of natural number between range - python\nURL: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/31483654/sum-of-natural-number-between-range\nPublished: 2015-09-16T03:28:59.500Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nThe question is how\n...\ndef sumNaturalNumbers(start, end, total_sum):\n li =\n...\n(start, end)\n sum = li[0] + li[-\n...\n]\n li.remove(li[0])\n li.remove(li[-1])\n total_sum += sum\n if len(li) == 0:\n return total_sum\n return sumNaturalNumbers(li[0], li[-1]+1, total_sum)\n...\nThere is a built-in function sum, that sums the inputted list:\n...\nsum(range(start, stop+1))\n\n>>> sum(range(1, 11)) #Sum of numbers between 1 and 10, inclusive\n55\n>>> sum(range(1, 101)) #Sum of numbers between 1 and 100, inclusive\n5050\n>>>\n...\nyou are supposed\n...\n, a simple\n...\ndef sumNaturalNumbers(start, end):\n if end == start:\n return start\n return end + sumNaturalNumbers(start, end-1)\n...\ndef sumNaturalNumbers(start, end):\n return (start + end) * (end - start + 1) // 2\n...\n>>> sumNaturalNumbers(1, 10)\n55\n...\n>>> sumNaturalNumbers(1, 100)\n5050\n...\nreturn sum(range(lower, upper))\n...\nsumBetween(1,1",
